[
  {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": "Search your name on Google and review the results a prospective client would see. Look for an old role, a broken personal website link, or a profile that competes with your current positioning. Record the page, the correction needed, and who can make it. Your digital footprint includes third-party bios and interviews, so some fixes will require contacting the publisher. Repeat the Google check after updates are live; a revised page may take time to appear in search results."
  }
]

[
  {
    "type": "heading",
    "content": "Turn the Value Proposition Into a Personal Brand Statement"
  },
  {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": "A personal brand statement is a short expression of your positioning. Use it to connect your audience, the change you help them make, and the way you work. Your unique value proposition is the underlying decision; the statement is language you can use in a bio, introduction, or website headline."
  },
  {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": "Test that statement with someone in your target audience. Ask them to describe who you help, what you help with, and what they would contact you about. If their answer differs from your intended offer, identify the word or assumption causing confusion. This is a practical test of brand messaging, not a vote on which sentence sounds most impressive."
  }
]

[
  {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": "For example, a hypothetical adviser might acknowledge a founder’s concern about handing decisions to managers, then explain how she defines decision rights and reviews them with the team. The concern supplies the emotive messaging; the process supplies the technical messaging. This approach can build trust because the reader can see both the problem and what the work involves."
  }
]

[
  {
    "type": "heading",
    "content": "Give Each Piece of Content a Job"
  },
  {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": "A useful personal branding content strategy identifies the question a piece will answer, who needs that answer, and the appropriate next step. Long form content can explain a difficult decision in depth. Short form posts can introduce one idea and direct interested readers to that fuller explanation. Choose the type of content around the question, rather than stretching every idea into every format."
  },
  {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": "For the hypothetical leadership adviser, a long form article might explain how to delegate a decision without losing accountability. Two social media posts could address common mistakes, while an email gives readers a question to discuss with their managers. Keep the same reasoning across formats. A strong personal brand becomes easier to recognize when its advice stays consistent, not when every channel repeats the same caption."
  },
  {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": "Personal branding also depends on relationships beyond publishing. Answer useful questions in a relevant online community, follow through on introductions, and give credit when someone else’s work informs yours. These actions build social capital through contribution. Treat “know like and trust factor” as shorthand for familiarity and confidence, not a formula that guarantees a sale."
  }
]

[
  {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": "Make the voice guidance concrete. Instead of asking a writer to sound “confident,” show an approved paragraph and explain the choices: direct language, specific examples, and no claims you cannot support. Note the core values that affect your business decisions and the expressions you would never use. This gives your brand personality a usable form and helps maintain brand consistency when several people create material for you."
  }
]
